# CERTIFICATE POINTS BEYOND H22 AND H24: MEMBERS ON A CYCLE.
#
# H22 (typing_natural_symbol) judges one member whose method passes the
# member itself back through the certificate of its argument; H24
# (typing_natural_pair) splits it into two members that pass each other.
# A point here splits it into Count members m0, ..., m(Count - 1) on a
# cycle: the method of m_i passes m_(i+1), and that of the last passes
# m0, each through the identity of the certificate the judgment gives its
# argument (`contest_hard_clauses.pl`, the section of H22 and H24, whose
# object triples, certificate and judgment are reused unchanged):
#
#     J x   = (tag x) ((base x) certificate (J (method x m0)))
#                     ((J x) (\i. \w. w))
#     m_i   = (true, \that. (identity_of (J that)) m_(i+1), false)
#
# Assumed the certificate for J m0, the assumption comes back after
# Count laps through the members.
#
# THE CLAIMS (class certificate_cycle): the templates of H22 and H24,
# member(1, natural), member(2, natural) and member(2, stream), all
# scored, proved by certificate_collapse extended to a cycle of any Count
# >= 1 members (`doc/proofs/certificate_cycles.md`,
# `osprey_certificate_cycle_collapse.md`,
# `individual_certificate_cycles_consistency.md`).
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - proving that lambda beta eta plus an equation is consistent by adding it
#   as a reduction rule and showing the union confluent, the Jacopini
#   technique of the collapse lemma the proofs above use: J. Kuper, On the
#   Jacopini Technique, Information and Computation 1997,
#   doi:10.1006/inco.1997.2655.
# - confirming an assumed certificate coinductively, by the equations: D.
#   Kozen, A. Silva, Practical Coinduction, Mathematical Structures in
#   Computer Science 2016, doi:10.1017/S0960129515000493.

#
# The template is a constructor of schema/template and the proof one of
# schema/proof. Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott
# list of bytes, imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "list")
λ nil cons _ _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "text") "cbor")
λ text_from_cbor _ _ _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. text_from_cbor λx. x)
λ text
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "integer") "cbor")
λ integer_from_cbor _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. integer_from_cbor λx. x)
λ integer
. omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "schema") "template")
λ _ _ member
. omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "schema") "proof")
λ proved _
. export
λ
. cons
  ( λ _cycle_claim
    . _cycle_claim (text "certificate_cycle") (text "scored") (member (integer 1) λ. text "natural") λ. proved λ. text "certificate_collapse"
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _cycle_claim
    . _cycle_claim (text "certificate_cycle") (text "scored") (member (integer 2) λ. text "natural") λ. proved λ. text "certificate_collapse"
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _cycle_claim
    . _cycle_claim (text "certificate_cycle") (text "scored") (member (integer 2) λ. text "stream") λ. proved λ. text "certificate_collapse"
  ) nil
